WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.522
For this guided practice, explain the three COUNT patterns without running anything first.

00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:09.602
COUNT star answers how many input rows are present.

00:00:09.602 --> 00:00:14.847
COUNT of a column answers how many non-null values that expression contains.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:19.592
COUNT DISTINCT answers how many unique non-null values occur.

00:00:19.592 --> 00:00:24.872
Try to create a small mental example with three rows, including one NULL and one repeated value.

00:00:24.872 --> 00:00:26.712
Predict what each COUNT would return.

00:00:26.712 --> 00:00:33.552
Then compare your explanation with the answer shown on the slide.

00:00:33.552 --> 00:00:34.412
This type of verbal reasoning is important.

00:00:34.412 --> 00:00:40.537
If you can explain the measure before you write the SQL,

00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:44.737
you are less likely to choose a count that answers the wrong question.

00:00:44.737 --> 00:00:48.922
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:48.922 --> 00:00:53.987
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:57.537
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:57.537 --> 00:01:01.682
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:01.682 --> 00:01:08.747
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:08.747 --> 00:01:12.917
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:17.622
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
